# Life Mode — the pepito screen-off "be present" feature (PLAN-lifemode.md).
# Both writers are system_app (per seapp_contexts user=system+seinfo=platform):
# the QS tile in XiaomiParts owns the master switch, the Pepito Tweaks screen
# in LineageParts owns the behavior knobs.
#
# That is the whole policy surface. Unlike gotweaks, no vendor_init or
# qti_init_shell grants are needed: there are no `on property:` triggers and no
# sysfs writes behind these properties. Everything Life Mode does it does
# through framework APIs (NotificationManager, NetworkPolicyManager,
# WifiManager) from inside system_app, which the base policy already covers.
get_prop(system_app, lifemode_prop)
set_prop(system_app, lifemode_prop)

# The pepito gate itself. LifeModeController.isSupported() reads
# ro.vendor.xiaomi.device (labeled vendor_xiaomi_prop, declared vendor_public_prop
# in property.te) to decide whether to enable the tile and register the screen
# listener at all - and system_app had no read grant for it. Under Enforcing that
# read is denied, SystemProperties.get() hands back "", isSupported() returns
# false, and Life Mode silently switches itself off on the one device it is for:
# no tile, no listener, no error anywhere except the avc line. Caught on the
# 2026-07-12 validation flash.
#
# Same lesson as gotweak_prop's vendor_init grant next door: declaring a property
# type public does NOT imply any given domain may read it. Every reader needs its
# own get_prop.
get_prop(system_app, vendor_xiaomi_prop)
